  "@type": "Note",
  "@id": "note:notion-145",
  "title": "About NewRoot",
  "author": "person:jane-brew:001",
  "status": "final",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "b-1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "About NewRoot"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-2",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "NewRoot is a national social impact organization that focuses on nurturing people and cultivating restorative learning spaces[1][2]. Formerly known as Umoja Student Development Corporation, NewRoot has been working for over 20 years to transform the way young people experience school and how schools serve society[5]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-3",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The organization's primary focus is on building deep social-emotional learning (SEL) skills in educators[5]. They achieve this through several key initiatives:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-4",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. Professional learning and adult accountability: NewRoot provides training, workshops, consultation, and coaching to help schools and districts implement whole-school SEL approaches[2]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-5",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. Accredited Seminar curriculum: They offer an equity-centered Tier 1 MTSS approach to post-secondary preparation, transition, and academic reflection[2]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-6",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. Restorative justice structures and practices: NewRoot established peace rooms in schools to change school culture and provide students with safe spaces to process feelings and conflicts[2]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-7",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "4. Self-awareness and contextual thinking: They help partners shift to restorative mindsets and create school environments where students' realities are seen, heard, and considered[2]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-8",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "NewRoot partners with schools and districts to develop customized solutions that address social-emotional wellness, equity, and restorative justice[2]. Their approach involves:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-9",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. Consultation to understand partners' needs"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-10",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. Wellness checks and assessments"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-11",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. Collaborative strategy development"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-12",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "4. Implementation of learning and coaching cycles"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-13",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "5. Measuring skill development, implementation, and impact[3]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-14",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The organization's work is guided by core values such as community, curiosity, dignity, relationship, and radical love[2]. NewRoot aims to have a lasting impact on young people by helping them explore post-secondary options, develop critical thinking skills, and prepare for college, career, and life success[2][5]."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-15",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Citations:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
